---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
---

<ProjectLayout
  title="Deep Q-Network for Autonomous Lunar Landing"
  subtitle="Teaching an RL agent to land on the Moon — from 2D prototypes to 3D physics simulations"
  tags={[
    { label: "PyTorch", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PyTorch" },
    { label: "D3QN", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Q-learning#Deep_Q-network" },
    { label: "DDPG", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deterministic_policy_gradient" },
    { label: "MuJoCo", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MuJoCo" },
    { label: "PER", href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Experience_replay" },
  ]}
>

  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>The Problem</h2>
    <p>
      Autonomous lunar landing is one of the hardest control problems in aerospace — the agent must
      manage fuel, attitude, and descent rate simultaneously while dealing with no atmosphere for
      aerodynamic braking. I wanted to see if a reinforcement learning agent could learn this from
      scratch, with no pre-programmed flight dynamics knowledge.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>Phase 1: 2D Prototype</h2>
    <p>
      I started with OpenAI Gymnasium's LunarLander-v2 environment — a simplified 2D problem with
      discrete thrust actions. I implemented a Dueling Double Deep Q-Network (D3QN) in PyTorch that
      achieved a &gt;95% landing success rate after training.
    </p>
    <div class="story-callout">
      <strong>Key insight:</strong> The dueling architecture — splitting the Q-value into state-value
      and advantage streams — dramatically improved learning stability compared to vanilla DQN.
    </div>

    <!-- Replace with your actual screenshots/videos -->
    <div class="story-media">
      <!--
      <img src="/assets/lunar-2d-training.png" alt="2D training reward curve" />
      <p class="story-caption">Training reward curve showing convergence after ~800 episodes</p>
      -->
      <p class="story-caption">Add training curves and 2D landing footage here: public/assets/</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section">
    <h2>Phase 2: Custom 3D MuJoCo Environment</h2>
    <p>
      The 2D environment was a great proof of concept, but real lunar landers operate in 3D with
      continuous thrust control. I built a custom rigid-body simulation in MuJoCo with accurate
      1.62 m/s² lunar gravity, modeling a lander with 5 individually controllable thrusters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The state space expanded to 13 dimensions: position (x, y, z), velocity, orientation quaternion,
      angular velocity, and remaining fuel mass. This was too complex for discrete actions, so I
      switched architectures.
    </p>

    <div class="story-grid">
      <!--
      <div class="story-media">
        <img src="/assets/mujoco-lander-model.png" alt="MuJoCo lander 3D model" />
        <p class="story-caption">Custom lander model in MuJoCo</p>
      </div>
      <div class="story-media">
        <img src="/assets/mujoco-env-overview.png" alt="MuJoCo environment overview" />
        <p class="story-caption">Landing pad and terrain setup</p>
